`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["At ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819"}),", ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/core/adapter.py",children:"adapter.py"})," defines Candidate as dict[str,str]. evaluate(batch,candidate,capture_traces) must construct the system and return aligned outputs/scores and, when requested, trajectories; make_reflective_dataset returns per-component JSON-serializable records. ProposalFn explicitly permits coupled updates. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/strategies/component_selector.py",children:"Component selection"})," offers one component in round-robin order or all keys."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/proposer/reflective_mutation/reflective_mutation.py",children:"mutation implementation"})," copies the parent and asserts each returned replacement key already exists (585–588); this assignment does not itself check membership in components_to_update or enforce semantic dependencies. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/proposer/merge.py",children:"Merge"})," requires identical component-key sets and takes whole component texts from descendants sharing an ancestor. When both descendants changed a component, executable code chooses the higher aggregate-score descendant, randomly breaking ties (159–190), despite an adjacent comment describing proportional random selection. It does not synthesize or type-check their combined workflow."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/core/state.py",children:"Candidate hashing"})," is SHA-256 over JSON-serialized sorted candidate items (37–39). This identifies text, not the builder, tool implementation, operation authority or external runtime. Existing cache warnings remain correct."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Proposed GameMap adaptation, unimplemented:"})," retain the GEPA map, e.g. tool_schema, workflow and evidence_policy, rather than claim GEPA supplies a graph language. Freeze a builder interface: validate_and_build(candidate, frozen_contract) returns either a plan or structured invalid_candidate reasons. Declare primitive operation IDs, argument/result schemas, dependency edges, sequential/parallel execution, bounded retries and error branches. Validate the mutation mask and coupled-schema compatibility before evaluation. Keep evaluator/permissions outside candidate text. Identify an executable candidate with component-text hash plus builder revision, primitive/schema manifest, dependency lock and fixed authority hash; keep experiment/trial identity separate. Store parent IDs and the chosen component provenance for merges."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{id:"2-gepa-metric-calls-depend-on-the-execution-path",children:"2. GEPA: “metric calls” depend on the execution path"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["EvaluationBatch has optional num_metric_calls. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/proposer/reflective_mutation/reflective_mutation.py",children:"Reflective evaluation"})," honors it for parents and children, falling back to batch length (372–376, 638–642). ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/core/engine.py",children:"Full validation"})," instead charges len(val_ids) with traces and len(uncached) otherwise (287–368). The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/core/state.py",children:"merge/cache evaluator"})," likewise returns requested/cache-miss example counts. Therefore num_metric_calls is not a universal accounting override for nested model calls or retries."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Reflective batch counts are incremented after a batch returns. The default ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/core/adapter.py",children:"batch adapter"})," evaluates pairs sequentially; if a later call raises after earlier external work, that path does not provide a complete spent-resource count. Batched reflection failure also triggers per-task retries (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/gepa-ai/gepa/blob/f1cfdee2856e9dc4b5b4dc5cc2e391d0aa20d819/src/gepa/proposer/reflective_mutation/reflective_mutation.py",children:"245–272"}),"); additional attempted work must be retained. These are static paths, not reproduced overspending."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Proposed control:"})," maintain an append-only dispatch/settlement ledger outside optimizer scores: candidate/trial/task/operation IDs; phase; reserved budget; attempts; observed model tokens, tool/render calls and wall time; charged/estimated/unknown cost; terminal status. Reconcile cancellation and late results. Keep valid task failure, invalid candidate, infrastructure failure, timeout, abstention and unknown outcome distinct, even if a declared optimization metric assigns some the same failure score. A missing usage report is unknown, not zero. Budget admission and total-cost reconciliation remain adapter/executor responsibilities."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{id:"3-meta-harness-a-concrete-interface-and-incomplete-coverage-boundary",children:"3. Meta-Harness: a concrete interface and incomplete-coverage boundary"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["At ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8"}),", the classification example’s ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/memory_system.py",children:"MemorySystem"})," requires predict(input) → (answer,metadata), learn_from_batch(results), get_state() → serialized string and set_state(state). Its ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/inner_loop.py",children:"loader"})," imports a module and instantiates the first inspected MemorySystem subclass (479–504). The outer ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/meta_harness.py",children:"validation"})," checks zero process exit status plus stdout containing OK when importing the candidate (192–215); that is narrower than validating the runtime interface and is already code execution, not safe static inspection."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/benchmark.py",children:"benchmark.py"})," requires complete configured seed groups per model/dataset/candidate (179–239), but aggregate-frontier construction averages accuracy over the dataset groups actually loaded, without requiring every configured dataset for each candidate (675–688). Its context-character axis instead averages only strictly positive context lengths and then truncates to an integer; these axes therefore use different inclusion rules. The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/meta_harness.py",children:"outer summary"})," similarly averages available datasets (529–540). Zero score is labeled “failed”; invalid imports disappear from the normal summary when other candidates are valid (232–244, 490–560). Preserve a complete manifest rather than treating those summaries as exhaustive failure denominators."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The Pareto axis is memory_context_chars, not total model cost. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/memory_system.py",children:"MemorySystem"})," tracks the last prompt per thread and defines context length in characters. The actual classification evaluation uses the last tracked call_llm prompt length minus test-input length, clamped to zero and integer-averaged (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/inner_loop.py",children:"inner_loop.py 439–469"}),"); this includes template text as well as memory, rather than serialized-state size or all prompts. Its evaluated metric does not call the default get_context_length path. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/blob/8123ccabe2b19fa1123090b2e5f1bacc20963ce8/reference_examples/text_classification/benchmark.py",children:"benchmark.py"})," separately sums available runtime, call and token fields, with missing fields defaulting to zero. Do not rename character efficiency as token or spending efficiency."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Proposed control:"})," content-address candidate code plus dependencies/configuration; record the exact exported class instead of relying on module names. Require complete prespecified dataset/seed coverage for promotion, report coverage separately, and retain every proposal/import/evaluation attempt. Validate interfaces without importing untrusted candidates; later runtime validation requires a separately authorized isolated environment."]}),`
