`,e.jsx(t.h3,{id:"harness-contrastive-task-local-edits-and-reusable-improvement-evidence",children:"Harness²: contrastive task-local edits and reusable improvement evidence"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14224v1",children:"October 9 paper, v1"}),", studies frozen-model harness adaptation on professional-work benchmarks, not game production. It proposes invariant and domain-specific edits, probes them on the same task, then composes a successor from execution contrasts. Those contrasts do not certify correctness. The next task starts from a common base harness; adopted components and lessons inform subsequent proposals rather than automatically replacing that base. Parallel width and sequential depth are distinct budget choices. The paper reports three shuffled streams and discusses selection regressions, shared incorrect premises, extra inference cost and uncertain transfer. Its leave-one-out completed-library analysis can access later tasks and is explicitly an offline reuse analysis, not an online gain estimate. These distinctions make it a useful comparison for changing a visual harness between project tasks without treating selected output as verified improvement."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Released surface."})," The author-linked ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/tree/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335",children:"repository snapshot"})," contains orchestration code, benchmark bridges and a deterministic demo. Its ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/blob/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335/README.md",children:"README"})," describes OpenCode/Codex executors and model-backed JobBench, LAB and WorkBuddy workflows. These need external dependencies and model access; none was installed or run here. Apache-2.0 covers the repository's own code, while third-party code/data retain their terms. Released code and paper results are separate evidence; this inspection does not establish that these exact bytes produced the paper's results."]}),`
